tt-bio-demo (0.7.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * The browser companion (`webview/`, `scripts/run-webview.sh`) is real and
    documented, not just tested and shelved: a bridge process
    (webview/bridge.py) streams the daemon's own event protocol to any
    number of browser tabs over Server-Sent Events, with a from-scratch
    client -- real point-cloud rendering of real fold coordinates, a
    quad/solo toggle that defaults to quad once more than one chip is
    available (matching the native booth), an affinity Q&A panel, real
    per-chip Tensix core-grid activity (the same vendored library and
    palette the native app uses), and real tt-smi-backed power/clock
    telemetry. Hardened against a security review: request handling is
    serialized against the one real daemon socket, Content-Length is
    bounds-checked and read is timeout-bounded, a non-loopback host now
    requires --auth-token/WEBVIEW_AUTH_TOKEN on every request, and a
    path-traversal fix in the static file server. Known, documented scope
    cuts remain (no ribbon/cartoon reveal, gallery shows targets seen live
    rather than the curated playlist, the easter egg stays unsurfaced) --
    see webview/README.md.
  * The booth no longer assumes a 1920x1080 screen. The side rail, the
    gallery, and the `?` help card all size themselves from the window's
    real width (`rail_width_for`, `help_card_width_for`), verified across
    1024x768 through 2560x1440 with a hardware-free headless-weston
    script. A 1280-wide window -- which used to need 1336px of content to
    fit -- fits with room to spare. Below a measured width the help card
    switches to a shorter but equally honest text rather than overflowing
    the screen.
  * The Tensix panel's per-chip animation now tracks the chip instead of
    only decorating it: tensix-viz 1.3.0 adds setActivity/setProgress, fed
    from real measured clock activity, the wire's own per-stage progress
    fraction (trunk's refinement cycles, diffusion's denoising steps), and
    -- preferred when available, with automatic fallback -- real per-chip
    power sampled from `tt-smi` every 1.5s. Floor/ceiling (15W idle, 90W
    folding) are this project's own measured numbers. Re-measured, not
    asserted fixed: a steady-state brightness swing of 69.4x between idle
    and fully active, against the ~0.3% frame-noise floor the earlier
    flicker fix measured. scripts/update-tensix-viz.sh now re-vendors the
    library and rewrites its own PROVENANCE.md attribution in one step.
  * Affinity Q&A: the three shipped question pairs carry real measured
    timings (10.1s/10.7s/8.3s); a planned six-question catalog expansion
    was reverted after a hardware test showed two of its new pairs scored
    the identical structure and two more always errored (scoring keys on
    the fold target, not on a per-question ligand, so a second ligand per
    protein was never actually distinguishable). The question text bobs
    per word with a colour cycle while nesso1 is actively scoring, on both
    the native UI and the browser companion, settling to plain text the
    instant a real answer lands and respecting GTK's reduced-motion
    setting. A question is now marked "recently asked" only once it
    actually reaches the daemon, not merely once the UI attempts to send
    it.
  * tt-bio bumped 0.8.0 -> 0.12.0 (four upstream releases absorbed in one
    pass). All seven playlist targets re-measured on real hardware: six
    are flat to modestly faster; albumin (HSA) is 12% faster with a real
    ~3.5-point pLDDT drop. The initial cause given for that shift did not
    hold up under review and has been corrected -- the real cause is not
    fully isolated; see `playlist/manifest.yaml`'s header for the complete,
    honest account. Commit counts and the model-family count (14 -> 15,
    adding af2ig) are refreshed on the docs site.
  * A real test-isolation bug is fixed: a unit test calling the daemon's
    real `main()` in-process could pin a stale weights-cache path into the
    test process's actual environment, breaking any hardware-integration
    test that happened to run afterward in the same session.
  * Doc fixes: INSTALL.md/README's stale tt-bio pin, playlist target count,
    and release curl example re-verified end to end against the real
    v0.6.1 release.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Mon, 05 Oct 2026 10:00:00 -0700

tt-bio-demo (0.6.1) noble; urgency=medium

  * v0.6.0 was tagged and released with an empty CI gate failure: the
    version bump left debian/changelog and the onepager PDF stamped with
    the previous version, so its own build-and-install gate failed before
    the release job ever ran, and the v0.6.0 GitHub Release was published
    with zero assets. Same code, same feature (affinity questions); this
    version exists only to carry real packages. v0.6.0 is left published
    and empty rather than backfilled, matching this project's own
    standing rule that a published version's contents never change after
    the fact -- the same call made for v0.5.5.

